1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,438
An imported data set keeps a copy of the other system's rows inside

2
00:00:05,438 --> 00:00:08,782
your site, and refreshes that copy on a schedule.

3
00:00:08,782 --> 00:00:12,795
This video shows how to set one up, refresh it by hand, read

4
00:00:12,795 --> 00:00:16,260
its history, and what happens when a refresh fails.

5
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:25,471
A live data set asks the other system every time a report reads it.

6
00:00:25,471 --> 00:00:30,840
An imported data set copies the rows into your site, and reports read the copy.

7
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:36,320
Reports are faster, and they keep working when the other system is down or slow.

8
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:39,464
The copy is replaced as a whole.

9
00:00:39,464 --> 00:00:44,251
A refresh publishes its rows only when every one has been written, and

10
00:00:44,251 --> 00:00:46,960
a refresh that fails changes nothing.

11
00:00:47,710 --> 00:00:51,763
So the question is only how fresh the copy needs to be, and

12
00:00:51,763 --> 00:00:54,070
the refresh settings answer it.

13
00:00:58,273 --> 00:01:02,862
Add a data set for the Sales API, name it Imported orders, and

14
00:01:02,862 --> 00:01:05,494
set its resource to slash orders.

15
00:01:05,494 --> 00:01:07,923
Under Access, choose Imported.

16
00:01:08,374 --> 00:01:10,437
A Refresh section appears.

17
00:01:10,437 --> 00:01:14,498
Refresh every says how many minutes pass between imports.

18
00:01:14,498 --> 00:01:17,722
Leave it empty to refresh only when you ask.

19
00:01:17,722 --> 00:01:20,752
Refresh reads is every row for a web API.

20
00:01:20,752 --> 00:01:25,071
And a policy says what to do when the source's fields change:

21
00:01:25,071 --> 00:01:30,164
add the new ones and carry on, or fail the refresh until you review them.

22
00:01:30,615 --> 00:01:32,988
The rest is as for a live data set:

23
00:01:32,988 --> 00:01:37,501
the connector's settings, then Discover fields, an identifier, and Save.

24
00:01:37,501 --> 00:01:41,899
The list says the first import is waiting to run, because a background

25
00:01:41,899 --> 00:01:44,155
task picks it up within a minute.

26
00:01:44,605 --> 00:01:47,735
Once it has run, the list shows the result:

27
00:01:47,735 --> 00:01:51,375
it succeeded, how many rows it published, and when.

28
00:01:55,572 --> 00:01:57,506
The source has new orders.

29
00:01:57,506 --> 00:02:00,949
Open Actions on the data set and click Refresh now.

30
00:02:00,949 --> 00:02:05,962
The site queues the refresh, and the background task runs it within a minute.

31
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:10,003
The list now shows more rows from the latest refresh.

32
00:02:10,003 --> 00:02:13,593
Reports read the new copy as soon as it is published.

33
00:02:14,045 --> 00:02:16,424
Open Actions and click History.

34
00:02:16,424 --> 00:02:21,309
Each entry shows when the refresh ran, who or what started it, whether

35
00:02:21,309 --> 00:02:26,645
it read every row, how many rows it read and published, and how long it took.

36
00:02:30,847 --> 00:02:33,211
Now the sales API is down.

37
00:02:33,211 --> 00:02:35,427
Click Refresh now again.

38
00:02:35,877 --> 00:02:38,912
The list shows the refresh failed, and why.

39
00:02:38,912 --> 00:02:43,248
But it still says how many rows the last good refresh published:

40
00:02:43,248 --> 00:02:46,097
a refresh that fails never removes rows.

41
00:02:46,547 --> 00:02:51,637
Preview still shows the rows, so reports keep working while you fix the source.

42
00:02:52,297 --> 00:02:58,200
The history keeps the failed entry with its error, next to the refreshes that worked.

43
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:02,157
When the API is back, the next refresh publishes again.

44
00:03:06,352 --> 00:03:11,607
For SQL Server and Elasticsearch, Refresh reads can be limited to only the

45
00:03:11,607 --> 00:03:14,366
rows changed since the last refresh.

46
00:03:14,366 --> 00:03:17,322
That is much lighter for a large table.

47
00:03:17,773 --> 00:03:21,407
It needs a field that grows when a row changes, such as a

48
00:03:21,407 --> 00:03:23,944
modification date or a version number.

49
00:03:23,944 --> 00:03:28,212
Pick it under Changed rows have a larger, and tick Identifier on the

50
00:03:28,212 --> 00:03:32,653
fields that identify a row, so changed rows replace their old versions.

51
00:03:33,105 --> 00:03:36,823
A row deleted at the source stays in the copy until the next

52
00:03:36,823 --> 00:03:38,795
refresh that reads every row.

53
00:03:39,246 --> 00:03:42,916
And when the source's fields change, the policy decides:

54
00:03:42,916 --> 00:03:47,001
add the new fields and keep the removed ones empty, or fail the

55
00:03:47,001 --> 00:03:51,146
refresh and keep the published rows until you review the fields.

56
00:03:53,146 --> 00:03:57,639
Import what must be fast and always available, refresh it on the schedule

57
00:03:57,639 --> 00:04:01,337
that fits, and read the history when something looks wrong.

58
00:04:01,337 --> 00:04:02,816
Thanks for watching.

